{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1612"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c135): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c135.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a1612"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge film-media/film","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/film-media__film.g3.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",38],["-","1970s"],["+","decades"],["=",1],["-","and"],["+","from"],["=",1],["-","1980s,"],["+","the"],["=",1],["+","late 1960s to "],["=",2],["+","1990s, the "],["=",28],["-","most"],["+","many"],["=",13],["+","Sometimes the role is a pair. In „The Odd Couple“ (1968), written by Neil Simon after his own Broadway hit, two divorced men share a New York apartment: the fussy hypochondriac Felix Ungar and the slovenly sportswriter Oscar Madison. They were played by Jack Lemmon and Walter Matthau, whose bickering chemistry was so perfect that they made about ten films together; thirty years later Simon reunited them for „The Odd Couple II“ (1998), shortly before both actors died.\n\n"],["=",622],["+","The blockbusters of the 1980s and 1990s could fuse actor and role just as surely. Tony Scott's „Top Gun“, released on 16 May 1986, put Tom Cruise in the cockpit of an F-14 as the cocky naval aviator Pete Mitchell, call sign „Maverick“; it was the year's biggest hit, and 36 years later Cruise returned to the part in „Top Gun: Maverick“ (2022). In Chris Columbus's comedy „Mrs. Doubtfire“ (1993), released in Germany with the subtitle „Das stachelige Kindermädchen“, Robin Williams plays a divorced voice actor who disguises himself as an elderly Scottish housekeeper to stay close to his children – one of the biggest successes of his career.\n\n"],["=",299],["+","\n| The Odd Couple | 1968 | Jack Lemmon and Walter Matthau | no |\n| Top Gun | 1986 | Tom Cruise | no |\n| Mrs. Doubtfire | 1993 | Robin Williams | no |"]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",34],["-","1970er-"],["+","Jahrzehnte"],["=",1],["-","und"],["+","von"],["=",1],["-","1980er-Jahre,"],["+","den"],["=",1],["+","späten 1960er- bis zu den 1990er-Jahren, "],["=",28],["-","die"],["+","viele"],["=",1],["-","meisten "],["=",10],["+","Manchmal ist die Rolle ein Paar. In „Ein seltsames Paar“ (1968), von Neil Simon nach seinem eigenen Broadway-Erfolg geschrieben, teilen sich zwei geschiedene Männer eine New Yorker Wohnung: der pedantische Hypochonder Felix Ungar und der schlampige Sportjournalist Oscar Madison. Gespielt wurden sie von Jack Lemmon und Walter Matthau, deren zänkische Chemie so perfekt war, dass sie rund zehn Filme zusammen drehten; dreißig Jahre später vereinte Simon sie für „Immer noch ein seltsames Paar“ (1998), kurz bevor beide Schauspieler starben.\n\n"],["=",606],["+","Die Blockbuster der 1980er- und 1990er-Jahre konnten Schauspieler und Rolle ebenso sicher verschmelzen. Tony Scotts „Top Gun“, am 16. Mai 1986 gestartet, setzte Tom Cruise als großspurigen Marineflieger Pete Mitchell mit dem Rufnamen „Maverick“ ins Cockpit einer F-14; es war der größte Erfolg des Jahres, und 36 Jahre später kehrte Cruise in „Top Gun: Maverick“ (2022) in die Rolle zurück. In Chris Columbus' Komödie „Mrs. Doubtfire – Das stachelige Kindermädchen“ (1993) spielt Robin Williams einen geschiedenen Synchronsprecher, der sich als ältliche schottische Haushälterin verkleidet, um seinen Kindern nahe zu sein – einer der größten Erfolge seiner Karriere.\n\n"],["=",291],["+","\n| Ein seltsames Paar | 1968 | Jack Lemmon und Walter Matthau | nein |\n| Top Gun | 1986 | Tom Cruise | nein |\n| Mrs. Doubtfire | 1993 | Robin Williams | nein |"]]}]},
